            0.083,
            0.744,
            0.122
          ],
          "content": "All clocks, old or modern, are based on some process repeating continuously, which can be used to mark equal intervals of time."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.141,
            0.735,
            0.28
          ],
          "content": "Modern clocks measure time using the same basic principle —Modern clocks measure time using the same basic principle — periodically repeating processes — but use tiny and very rapidperiodically repeating processes — but use tiny and very rapid vibrations either from a quartz crystal (quartz clocks) or from somevibrations either from a quartz crystal (quartz clocks) or from some specific atoms (atomic clocks). While Huygens’ early pendulumspecific atoms (atomic clocks). While Huygens’ early pendulum clocks could gain or lose 10 seconds each day, today’s atomic clocksclocks could gain or lose 10 seconds each day, today’s atomic clocks are so precise that they lose only one second in millions of years! Scientists are constantly searching for even better ways to measure time with greater accuracy."
        },
